The perfect-tree property gives two direct links at every parent. Its left child points to its right child. Its right child points to the left child of the parent's `next` node, when that neighbor exists.

The links are set before visiting the children, so the next level can use the links already created above it. This code changes the `next` fields in the original nodes.

```java
class Solution {
    public Node connect(Node root) {
        if (root == null || root.left == null) {
            return root;
        }

        root.left.next = root.right;

        if (root.next != null) {
            root.right.next = root.next.left;
        }

        connect(root.left);
        connect(root.right);

        return root;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`, with one visit per node.
Space complexity: `O(log n)` for the recursion stack in a perfect tree.

A marker node separates one level from the next in the queue. The `previous` pointer remembers the last real node from the current level. When another real node arrives, the previous one points to it. At the marker, `previous` is cleared so levels do not join together.

The marker is only used inside the queue and is not part of the tree. For example, after processing `2` and then `3`, the link becomes `2.Next = 3`, while `3.Next` remains `nil`.

```go
func connect(root *Node) *Node {
	if root == nil {
		return nil
	}

	queue := []*Node{root, nil}
	var previous *Node

	for len(queue) > 0 {
		current := queue[0]
		queue = queue[1:]

		if current == nil {
			previous = nil
			if len(queue) > 0 {
				queue = append(queue, nil)
			}
			continue
		}

		if previous != nil {
			previous.Next = current
		}
		previous = current

		if current.Left != nil {
			queue = append(queue, current.Left)
		}
		if current.Right != nil {
			queue = append(queue, current.Right)
		}
	}

	return root
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`, as every tree node enters and leaves the queue once.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the queue, including the level marker.

This keeps a stack instead of using recursion. Before pushing children, each node creates the two links needed below it. The stack visits the left side first, so a child's parent's `next` link is already available when that child is handled.

For a parent, the first child link is always `left.next = right`. The second link crosses to the next parent's left child when `parent.next` exists. Leaf nodes need no work.

If the input is empty, the method returns `null`; a one-node tree also stays unchanged with `next` set to `null`.

```javascript
class Solution {
    connect(root) {
        if (root === null) {
            return null;
        }

        const stack = [root];

        while (stack.length > 0) {
            const current = stack.pop();

            if (current.left !== null) {
                current.left.next = current.right;

                if (current.next !== null) {
                    current.right.next = current.next.left;
                }

                stack.push(current.right);
                stack.push(current.left);
            }
        }

        return root;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n)` since each node is removed from the stack once.
Space complexity: `O(log n)` for the stack in a perfect tree.

A queue makes the level boundaries easy to track. For each level, remove its nodes one by one and connect each node to the next node still waiting in that same level. Children are added after the current level is counted.

The last node is left with `next = nullptr`. The empty tree check also matters because there is nothing to process in that case.

Dry run for a small tree:

```text
Level 1: 1 -> null
Level 2: 2 -> 3 -> null
Level 3: 4 -> 5 -> 6 -> 7 -> null
```

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    Node* connect(Node* root) {
        if (root == nullptr) {
            return nullptr;
        }

        std::queue<Node*> nodes;
        nodes.push(root);

        while (!nodes.empty()) {
            int levelSize = static_cast<int>(nodes.size());

            for (int i = 0; i < levelSize; ++i) {
                Node* current = nodes.front();
                nodes.pop();

                if (i + 1 < levelSize) {
                    current->next = nodes.front();
                } else {
                    current->next = nullptr;
                }

                if (current->left != nullptr) {
                    nodes.push(current->left);
                }
                if (current->right != nullptr) {
                    nodes.push(current->right);
                }
            }
        }

        return root;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n)` because every node is processed once.
Space complexity: `O(n)` in the worst case for the queue.
